  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; paragraph preceding equation (1.2)",
      "anchor": "one-dimensional pipe at constant velocity",
      "observation": "The example concerns a contaminant transported by fluid in one spatial dimension with constant velocity ū; q(x,t) denotes its concentration or density."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equation (1.2)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + ū q_x(x,t) = 0",
      "observation": "The scalar advection equation has constant transport coefficient ū."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; explanation following equation (1.1)",
      "anchor": "subscripts are used to denote partial derivatives",
      "observation": "The subscripts t and x denote partial differentiation with respect to time and space. The general system uses q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝ^m; the scalar case has m = 1 and a real scalar coefficient."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; section 1.1, equation (1.8)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0",
      "observation": "The differential conservation-law form differentiates the composed flux with respect to space, and f is called the flux function."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF pages 25–26; printed pages 3–4; sentence spanning the page break",
      "anchor": "linear flux function f(q) = Aq",
      "observation": "The constant-coefficient linear system (1.1) is identified as a conservation law with linear flux."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; section 1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "for any two points x₁ and x₂",
      "observation": "The integral conservation law states d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t))."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; paragraph immediately following equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "can change only due to the flux past the endpoints",
      "observation": "The integral represents the amount of a conserved quantity between the endpoints. For the contaminant example, the source explicitly identifies conservation of total contaminant mass and the flux f(q) = ūq as the basis for equation (1.2)."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; end of the contaminant-mass paragraph",
      "anchor": "must also contain source terms",
      "observation": "If contaminant mass is not conserved, for example because of chemical reactions, the conservation law must include source terms."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; section 1.1.2, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "Deriving the differential conservation law (1.8) from the integral form (1.10) requires sufficient smoothness; the source emphasizes that solutions can instead contain discontinuities."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "x and t are real spatial and temporal variables, and q(x,t) is a scalar concentration or density; this specializes the general real-valued system to m = 1 (printed page 1, explanation of (1.1) and paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
      "ū is a fixed real transport velocity, constant in space and time in this example (printed page 1, constant-coefficient scalar discussion and paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
      "f is the scalar flux function with defining formula f(r) = ūr, where r denotes its scalar argument rather than a new physical quantity (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph, f(q) = ūq).",
      "x₁ and x₂ are arbitrary spatial endpoints in the integral balance (printed page 4, sentence preceding (1.10))."
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "A contaminant is carried by fluid through a one-dimensional pipe at constant velocity ū (printed page 1, paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
      "Contaminant mass is conserved during transport; the selected homogeneous equation excludes the mass production or loss for which the source requires source terms (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph).",
      "For passage from the integral balance to a classical differential equation, q and the composed flux f(q) must be sufficiently smooth (printed page 4, opening of section 1.1.2)."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "The contaminant flux is f(r) = ūr (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph).",
      "Conservation of contaminant mass with this flux yields the scalar advection equation q_t(x,t) + ūq_x(x,t) = 0, identified by the source as equation (1.2) (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph; printed page 1, (1.2)).",
      "Specializing the stated integral balance to this flux gives d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q(x,t) dx = ūq(x₁,t) − ūq(x₂,t); this is a direct substitution into (1.10), rather than a separately displayed source formula (printed page 4, (1.10) and contaminant paragraph).",
      "If contaminant mass is not conserved, the model must also contain source terms (printed page 4, end of contaminant paragraph)."
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "q denotes concentration or density of contaminant, not the fluid velocity (printed page 1, paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
      "Subscripts t and x denote partial derivatives with respect to time and space (printed page 1, explanation following (1.1)).",
      "In (1.8), f(q(x,t))_x is the spatial derivative of the composed flux (printed page 3, (1.8)).",
      "The integral of q between endpoints represents contaminant mass in that interval; its change equals the flux at x₁ minus the flux at x₂ (printed page 4, (1.10) and its explanatory paragraph)."
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The selected paragraph explicitly refers back to the scalar constant-velocity advection example, so its ū and q inherit their roles from page 1 (printed page 4, reference to (1.2); printed page 1, paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
      "The flux identification is a scalar instance of the preceding observation that constant-coefficient linear systems have flux f(q) = Aq (printed pages 3–4, sentence spanning the page break).",
      "The source distinguishes integral conservation from its classical differential expression when solutions lack sufficient smoothness (printed page 4, opening of section 1.1.2)."
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Local conservation and endpoint signs",
        "details": [
          "Mass inside an interval may change through endpoint transport even though contaminant mass is conserved; conservation does not assert that every interval has constant mass (printed page 4, explanation of (1.10)).",
          "The balance uses f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)), fixing the order and sign of the endpoint terms (printed page 4, (1.10))."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Exact linear flux",
        "details": [
          "The source specifies the exact flux f(q) = ūq, with no additive constant (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph).",
          "The same constant ū appears as the transport coefficient in equation (1.2) and as the multiplier in the flux (printed page 1, (1.2); printed page 4, contaminant paragraph)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Physical scope and exceptions",
        "details": [
          "The selected claim concerns scalar contaminant transport under constant fluid velocity (printed page 1, paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
          "Chemical reactions are an example of a mechanism requiring source terms when contaminant mass fails to be conserved (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph)."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Preserve the exact flux formula f(q) = ūq and its identification with the contaminant advection equation (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph; printed page 1, (1.2)).",
    "Preserve the scalar concentration or density interpretation of q and the constant velocity interpretation of ū (printed page 1, paragraph preceding (1.2)).",
    "Preserve the equation q_t + ūq_x = 0 with the same coefficient as the flux (printed page 1, (1.2); printed page 4, contaminant paragraph).",
    "Any included integral formulation must preserve the endpoint difference in (1.10), specialized using f(q) = ūq (printed page 4, (1.10) and contaminant paragraph).",
    "Do not treat conservation as constant mass in each fixed interval: the source permits changes through endpoint flux (printed page 4, explanation following (1.10)).",
    "Do not assert an unqualified classical differential consequence for nonsmooth q; the source explicitly conditions the integral-to-differential derivation on sufficient smoothness (printed page 4, section 1.1.2).",
    "Retain the qualification that nonconservation of contaminant mass requires source terms (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph)."
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The exact regularity assumptions are unspecified.",
      "evidence": "Printed page 4, section 1.1.2 requires q and f(q) to be sufficiently smooth without naming a function space or derivative order.",
      "impact": "A precise classical formulation must choose adequate regularity assumptions, but should distinguish that choice from the source's explicit wording."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The physical example does not specify a pipe domain, time interval, boundary conditions, or initial data.",
      "evidence": "Printed page 1 introduces a one-dimensional pipe and equation (1.2), while the general system discussion gives real space and time variables; printed page 4 supplies an interval balance without boundary or initial conditions.",
      "impact": "The passage supports the flux and governing equation, not a particular initial-boundary-value problem or an existence or uniqueness assertion."
    },
    {
      "issue": "No explicit sign or nonvanishing restrictions are imposed on ū or q.",
      "evidence": "Printed page 1 describes constant velocity and concentration or density, and printed page 4 specifies f(q) = ūq, without inequalities.",
      "impact": "Nonnegative concentration, positive velocity, or nonzero velocity would be additional formal restrictions rather than explicit hypotheses of this passage."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "For a contaminant carried through a one-dimensional pipe at constant velocity ū, q(x,t) is its concentration or density and the flux is exactly f(q) = ūq (printed page 1, paragraph preceding (1.2); printed page 4, contaminant paragraph). Conservation of contaminant mass gives the advection equation q_t + ūq_x = 0, with the classical differential interpretation subject to the source's sufficient-smoothness proviso (printed page 1, (1.2); printed page 4, sections 1.1.1–1.1.2). The corresponding interval balance is the rate of change of interval mass equal to ūq(x₁,t) minus ūq(x₂,t), obtained by substituting the stated flux into (1.10). If contaminant mass is not conserved, source terms are required (printed page 4, contaminant paragraph)."
}
